    <!-- Unit Overview -->
    <section class="welcome-section">
        <h2><i class="fa-solid fa-compass section-icon"></i> Unit Overview</h2>
        <p>of Computer Science Unit Foundation Concepts The core focus is on Data Integrity and Relational Modelling. This unit explores how data is structured as a collection of tables (entities) to ensure consistency and reduce redundancy. Key themes include Atomic Data, Functional Dependencies (Partial vs. Transitive), and the use of Declarative Programming (SQL) to manage and retrieve information.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- Syllabus Links -->
    <section class="content-section">
        <h2><i class="fa-solid fa-bookmark section-icon"></i> What You'll Learn</h2>
        <div class="learning-points">
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <strong>A3.1.1</strong> — Features (Keys, Tuples, Attributes), Benefits (Concurrency control, ACID reliability), and Limitations (Hierarchical handling, Object-relational impedance mismatch, Rigid schemas).
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>A3.2.1/2</strong> — Database Schemas (Conceptual, Logical, Physical) and ERD construction including Modality (Optional/Mandatory) and Cardinality.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>A3.2.5/6</strong> — Normalization stages: 1NF (Atomicity), 2NF (No partial-key dependency), and 3NF (No transitive dependency).
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>A3.2.7</strong> — Evaluation of Denormalization for performance in read-intensive applications.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>A3.3.1</strong> — -3: SQL Programming: DDL (CREATE, ALTER, DROP) and DML (SELECT, JOIN, WHERE, LIKE, ORDER BY, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE).
                </li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </section>

