    <!-- Watch Out For -->
    <section class="exam-section" style="background: linear-gradient(135deg, #fff3cd 0%, #ffe5a0 100%); border-left-color: #ffc107;">
        <h2><i class="fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation section-icon" style="color: #856404;"></i> Common Misconceptions</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Scenario Neglect:</strong> Candidates often identify general benefits (e.g., "scalability") without connecting them to the specific requirements of the provided scenario (Textbook Tip).</li>
            <li><strong>Modality vs. Cardinality:</strong> Confusion between the maximum number of instances (Cardinality) and the minimum required instances (Modality).</li>
            <li><strong>Normalization vs. Sorting:</strong> Viewing normalization as a way to "tidy" data rather than a strict mathematical process to enforce functional dependencies.</li>
            <li><strong>Object-Relational Mismatch:</strong> Believing that OOP classes and Database tables are identical structures, ignoring the lack of "By-Reference" attributes in relational models.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

